"""Two-process HTTP acceptance for the restricted PostgreSQL runtime.

Dependencies: pytest, psycopg 3, argon2-cffi, OpenSSL CLI, a disposable
PostgreSQL database, and an ``atermius-server`` binary with the restricted PG
runtime wired in.
This suite is intentionally opt-in and never starts Cargo itself. Example:

    ATERMIUS_RUN_PG_HTTP_ACCEPTANCE=1 \
    ATERMIUS_TEST_PG_URL=postgresql://.../atermius_test_sync_http \
    ATERMIUS_PG_HTTP_SERVER_BIN=/path/to/atermius-server \
    pytest -q integration/test_pg_sync_http_acceptance.py

The startup contract expected by this fixture is:
``ATERMIUS_SYNC_BACKEND=postgres``, ``ATERMIUS_SYNC_PG_URL``,
``ATERMIUS_SYNC_PG_SCHEMA``, ``ATERMIUS_PG_INSTANCE_SIGNING_KEY_FILE``,
``ATERMIUS_PG_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_KEY_FILE``, a shared
``ATERMIUS_MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE``, plus a unique local
``ATERMIUS_DATA_DIR`` and ``ATERMIUS_BIND`` for each process.

This exercises the restricted PostgreSQL runtime: both processes use the same
PG schema for bootstrap and invited account registration, account/device/Vault
reads, provisioned-user prelogin/password login/refresh, session
authentication, sync writes/reads, instance identity, and Vault deletion
grace/cancel/purge while retaining separate local SQLite data directories.
The runtime also permits health, instance, and logout routes plus exact mobile
push endpoint register/delete paths. This fixture deliberately removes provider
credentials, so registration returns ``PUSH_DELIVERY_DISABLED`` without storing
the supplied token; endpoint deletion and revoke cleanup still run through PG.
Other unadapted operations remain blocked with HTTP 503 ``PG_SYNC_SCOPE_LIMITED``.

No test endpoint is needed for the revoke race. An external transaction holds
the session row with ``SELECT ... FOR UPDATE``; the test observes HTTP logout
on node A waiting behind that barrier, then observes the same-session push on
node B queued behind node A before committing the barrier.
"""

